patients each year and over 118 inpatient and outpatient clinical programs. The organization conducts ground-breaking research through its 11 research centres and provides expert training to over 13,000 health care professionals and scientists. In addition, CAMH delivers education programs to over 40,000 healthcare professionals each year and advocates on public policy issues at all levels of government. The successful applicant must hold an MD degree, must be Board certified or Board Eligible in Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada Specialty, and must be eligible for medical or academic licensure with the College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario. \u00a0The successful candidate with Addiction Medicine training is preferred but not necessary as well as a record of excellence in scholarship. \u00a0The successful candidate will have a scholarly interest in collaborative and integrated care delivery, measurement-based care and the use of digital technology in facilitating healthcare access and experience working collaboratively with clinicians on program design, research projects and research implementation. The successful candidate will ideally have experience and strong interest in collaborative care for the treatment of concurrent disorders. The salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The salary range of this position exceeds $200,000 CAD per annum. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply by October 15, 2026. \u00a0Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. \u00a0 Should you be interested in this opportunity, please forward a one-page statement of interest, curriculum vitae and the names and contact information of three referees via email to: Dr. Leslie Buckley Chief, Addictions Division The Centre for Addiction and Mental Health 1001 Queen Street West, Toronto, ON Tel: 416-535-8501 e-mail: leslie.buckley@camh.ca For more information on the University of Toronto, Department of Psychiatry, please visit http://www.psychiatry.utoronto.ca . For more information about Centre for Addiction and Mental Health (CAMH), please visit https://www.camh.ca/ . CAUTION: This ad is \u201cposted only\u201d to the U of T affiliated hospital job board. Please see the information directly above for the application instructions; applications submitted via the \"Apply Now\" button on this page will NOT be considered for this position. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Psychology, Counseling, Public Policy, Academic, Healthcare, Legal, Government, Education Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: All Opportunities, Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Director%2C-Academic-Human-Resources-ON/606680217/": {"title": "Director, Academic Human Resources", "description": "Director, Academic Human Resources Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Director, Academic Human Resources Date Posted: 10/02/2026 Req ID: 50406 Faculty/Division: Temerty Faculty of Medicine Department: Human Resources, Academic Campus : St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About Us The Temerty Faculty of Medicine at the University of Toronto is at the centre of one of North America\u2019s largest biomedical research, education and clinical care networks. With 14 fully affiliated research-intensive hospitals \u2014 and dozens more community and clinical care sites \u2014 Temerty Medicine offers unparalleled opportunities in Canada\u2019s most dynamic city for our more than 9,000 faculty and staff and 7,000 learners at all levels. Your Opportunity As Director, Academic Human Resources, you will provide strategic leadership, management, and vision for academic Human Resources services across the Temerty Faculty of Medicine. Serving as a vital bridge between the Faculty and university-wide leadership, you will inspire and mentor a high-performing administrative team who deliver frontline counsel and guidance on academic policies across more than 40 academic and administrative units. Reporting to the Chief Administrative Officer and acting as a key strategic partner to the Office of the Dean, your team will expertly guide faculty leadership through the complex appointment, review, and promotion lifecycle for our extensive network of over 9,000 faculty members. Along with advising on appointment offers for senior academic administrators and designing and implementing impactful onboarding experiences for chairs and directors, you will champion the Faculty's Academic Strategic Plan. Leveraging your operational insights, you will provide strategic support for new initiatives, task forces, and committees, playing an instrumental role in driving timely outcomes for key deliverables. Through this work, you will advance our commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion, helping to shape the Faculty's long-term strategic vision. Essential Qualifications Graduate degree or equivalent combination of education and experience Minimum ten years senior administrative experience within a university setting, including a deep familiarity with the academic HR life cycle, tenure process, and University academic HR norms Demonstrated experience in liaising with senior leaders, responding to and handling matters of a complex, confidential and sensitive nature Demonstrated professional experience in understanding complex issues related to organizational policies and practices Proven experience leading change and driving results through process evaluation and redesign Proven experience developing, leading, and mentoring high-functioning teams Experience interpreting human resources policies, legislation, and guidelines Demonstrated success in implementing equity and diversity initiatives across a complex multi-sector environment Data-driven with experience using various tools, performance metrics and approaches for analysis, decision-making and reporting Strong technical skills, including knowledge of the University of Toronto\u2019s Administrative Management System (AMS) and a facility for working with project management tools and keeping current with new and emerging technologies Strong leadership skills with ability to engage, secure support, commitment and results from others Outstanding interpersonal, communication and presentation skills Demonstrated knowledge of HR best practices and demonstrated commitment to the promotion of equity and inclusiveness in the workplace and the ability to work effectively with all constituents in a highly diverse community Ability to influence and present \u2018win/win\u2019 solutions to effect change and problem-solve in a highly collegial setting Strong integrative skills related to understanding cross functional implications and interrelationships of issues and decisions Proven ability to work with a high degree of independence solving complex problems Must be able to move complex initiatives forward in situations characterized by high levels of ambiguity and political sensitivity Must exhibit a high degree of diplomacy and tact in all situations Assets (Nonessential) Deep knowledge of the academic HR life cycle for clinical (MD) and non-clinical faculty, strongly preferred Understanding of University governance, educational priorities, policy formation, legal obligations, reporting requirements and planning protocol Closing Date: 10/23/2026,11:59PM ET Employee Group: Salaried Personnel Subarea: PM Appointment Type : Budget - Continuing Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: PM 7 -- Hiring Zone: $145,219 - $169,423 -- Broadband Salary Range: $145,219 - $242,032 Job Category: Administrative / Managerial Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: HR Manager, Human Resources Director, HR, Project Manager, Manager, Human Resources, Technology, Management Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Lab-Coordinator-%28term%29-ON/606698017/": {"title": "Lab Coordinator (term)", "description": "Lab Coordinator (term) Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Lab Coordinator (term) Date Posted: 10/02/2026 Req ID: 50416 Faculty/Division: Faculty of Arts & Science Department: Dept of Psychology Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Position Number: 00051750 Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About us: The Faculty of Arts & Science is the heart of Canada\u2019s leading university and one of the most comprehensive and diverse academic divisions in the world. The strength of Arts & Science derives from our combined teaching and research excellence in the humanities, sciences and social sciences across 29 departments, seven colleges and 46 interdisciplinary centres, institutes and programs. We can only realize our mission with the dedication and excellence of engaged staff and faculty. The diversity of opportunities and perspectives within the Faculty reflect the local and global landscape and the need for curiosity, innovative thinking and collaboration. At Arts & Science, we take pride in our legacy of innovation and discovery that has changed the way we think about the world. Established in 1927, the Department of Psychology has for more than a century been a top choice for those pursuing a career in psychological research. The combined expertise of our faculty spans all major areas of psychology and reflects a wide range of approaches to the study of mind, brain and behaviour. Our department comprises a diverse and internationally recognized faculty, first-rate laboratory facilities anda tradition of cutting-edge, collaborative research in an inspiring intellectual atmosphere. The Einstein Lab (Dr. Gillian Einstein) studies how the structure and function of the brain is influenced by the context of people\u2019s lives, especially sex and gender. These influences include hormones and genes, as well as culture, social environment and personal life experiences. In turn, these factors can influence our health and successful aging (https://einsteinlab.ca/). Your opportunity: Under the supervision of the Principal Investigator (PI), the Lab Coordinator is responsible for the smooth day-to-day operation of the lab, involving the acquisition of data on cognition and brain response. The incumbent ensures participants and/or testing rooms are not double-booked, forms are available to sign and payments/participation credits are processed. Duties include but are not limited to recruiting participants; preparing test materials; scheduling andkeeping track of participants\u2019 appointments and their compensation; interacting with research assistants about their duties. The Lab Coordinator is also responsible for general maintenance of the research lab; ensuring supplies are adequately stocked; compiling receipts and forms; ensuring reference libraries are maintained; lab manuals updated and maintained; files/website updated; back up information weekly; contacting departmental computer staff to ensure programs and computers run properly. Additionally, the incumbent performs other administrative duties such as maintaining a tidy environment and providing administrative support to PI, assisting with ethics protocols and grants, and assisting graduate students with participant testingand data collating. Note: this is a two-year term opportunity. Your responsibilities will include: Proofing and posting adverts to recruit applicants Preparing consent forms using templates Disseminating and collecting researchsurveys and questionnaires Generating reports Coordinating meeting schedules, agendas, materials, action and follow-up items Responding to enquiries within the defined scope of the role and redirecting as appropriate Recording or verifying transactions on a single account Providing orientation to others on work procedures and practices Essential Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree or acceptable combination of equivalent experience. Minimum two (2) years of relevant experience in a research lab or as a lab coordinator with knowledge of research methodology. Experience working with participants and collecting data/samples. Experience in trouble-shooting various technologies along with website maintenance. Experience interacting with participants and students of diverse backgrounds. Strong computer skills including with Microsoft Word, Excel, PowerPoint. Strong interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Sound time-management, organizational skills, and ability to multi-task. Demonstrated ability to work under pressure to meet multiple competing deadlines. Strong attention to detail, ability to use sound judgment, and proven ability to work within a team. Assets (Nonessential): Demonstrated knowledge in SPSS, Matlab, EPrime, BrainVoyager, and WordPress preferred. To be successful in this role you will be: Communicator Efficient Goal oriented Multi-tasker Organized Problem solver For internal staff, a copy of the detailed job description is available upon request from the Faculty of Arts and Science HR Office by emailing: adminhr.artsci@utoronto.ca . Closing Date: 10/19/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Grant - Term Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 07 -- $62,646 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $80,116. Pay scale and job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay Equity Maintenance Protocol. Job Category: Research Administration & Teaching Divisional HR Office Email: adminhr.artsci@utoronto.ca Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Job descriptions are available upon request for internal applicants. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Laboratory, Administrative Assistant, Equity, Science, Administrative, Research, Finance Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Data-Analyst%2C-Teaching-&-Learning-ON/606706417/": {"title": "Data Analyst, Teaching & Learning", "description": "Data Analyst, Teaching & Learning Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Data Analyst, Teaching & Learning Date Posted: 10/02/2026 Req ID: 50419 Faculty/Division: VP & Provost Department: Vice-Provost, Teaching & Learning Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Position Number: 00051167 Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About us: The Office of the Vice-Provost, Teaching & Learning (OVPTL) (formerly known as Office of the Vice-Provost: Innovation in Undergraduate Education) supports ongoing and new efforts to enhance the quality and expand the reach of high-impact teaching and learning practices at the University of Toronto. To support teaching and learning across the University, the OVPTL maintains a robust profile related to five key priorities: 1. Teaching Awards & Grants: Recognizing and funding staff and faculty who demonstrate commitment to innovation and excellence in teaching practices. 2. Curriculum Development: Working with institutional and divisional stakeholders to develop and assess course and program-level outcomes. 3. Teaching Initiatives: Stewarding the development of institutional frameworks and resources for innovative teaching practices, such as experiential learning, undergraduate research and generative artificial intelligence in teaching and learning. 4. Digital Transformation: Building and supporting systems that enable faculty, staff and students to achieve their academic goals. 5. Analytics: Leveraging data to enhance program planning, decision-making and student success. Your opportunity: Reporting to the Manager, Student Success Analytics, the Data Analyst, Teaching & Learning, will be part of a dynamic, collaborative, and innovative group focused on enhancing undergraduate student academic success at the University. By utilizing existing data tools and creating customized analyses and visualizations, you will identify areas of opportunity to support student retention, progression, and graduation for undergraduate students at the University of Toronto. Current priorities include establishing and supporting a sustainable institutional reporting and analytics framework, enabling the University to use student engagement data for institutional planning, student success initiatives, and unit-level decision-making. This work will create institutional reporting capabilities that deliver consistent, aggregated reporting on student engagement by division; strengthen existing student success analytics by integrating engagement indicators with retention, progression, and graduation data; and develop a standard assessment\u00a0and planning package using a common baseline set of reports. The role will also contribute to other institutional projects that support the teaching and learning environment, potentially including the University\u2019s Learning Analytics efforts using data from the learning management system (Canvas, known as Quercus at U of T), and other projects that provide key data insights to instructors, staff and academic leaders to support data-informed decisions that enhance teaching and learning. Across projects, the Analyst will consult with specialists on the data-driven design of student learning initiatives producing reports and analyses, and will have the opportunity to build new data tools that improve the student learning experience. The ideal candidate is comfortable working with stakeholders to understand their needs, brings creative approaches to interpreting learning data through analysis and visualization. This includes experience querying databases (SQL) coding using a programming language (e.g., R or Python) and a data visualization tool (e.g., Tableau or Power BI). Your responsibilities will include: Creating and producing visual images on scientific data and research Analyzing and developing program scripts to extract, reformat and analyze data Developing and maintaining a dashboard for quick access to key performance indicators Communicating and presenting results to technical and non-technical audiences Analyzing complex data that requires great precision and attention to many details Generating reports Coordinating tasks for projects and other strategic initiatives with stakeholders Essential Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree or acceptable combination of equivalent experience. Minimum five years of relevant experience supporting data-driven decision-making by conducting detailed data analysis, producing complex statistical information, and analyzing large datasets, preferably in a postsecondary education or research-intensive institution 2+ Years experience developing visualizations using Tableau or Power BI Experience developing and maintaining a dashboard of key performance indicators. Experience and intermediate skills in writing/coding programming scripts (e.g. Python, SQL) to extract, reformat, and analyze data. Experience effectively synthesizing information Experience training on research methods and data analysis Experience working with and manipulating complex data sets using Excel, Tableau and R Experience with data analysis. Familiarity with Microsoft Office products, and advanced skills in Microsoft Excel. Strong oral and written communication and presentation skills. Excellent organizational and time-management skills, with the ability to adapt to new and changing priorities Project management skills with demonstrated ability to meet deadlines and to manage a set of competing priorities. Demonstrated strong client service orientation and commitment to fostering equity, diversity and inclusion in a professional environment. To be successful in this role you will be: Meticulous Multi-tasker Organized Persuasive Problem solver Self-directed Closing Date: 10/19/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Budget - Continuing Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 13 -- $89,794 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $114,832. Pay scale and job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay Equity Maintenance Protocol. Job Category: Information Technology (IT) Divisional HR Office Email: Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Job descriptions are available upon request for internal applicants. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Data Analyst, Database, SQL, Curriculum Development, Training, Data, Technology, Education Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Dentist-in-Chief-Department-of-Paediatric-Dentistry%2C-Hospital-for-Sick-Children-ON/606707217/": {"title": "Dentist-in-Chief", "description": "Dentist-in-Chief - Department of Paediatric Dentistry, Hospital for Sick Children Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Dentist-in-Chief - Department of Paediatric Dentistry, Hospital for Sick Children Date Posted: 10/02/2026 Closing Date: 10/30/2026 , 11:59PM ET Req ID: 50422 Job Category: Faculty - Clinical Faculty/Division: Faculty of Dentistry Department: Faculty of Dentistry Hospital Affiliation: Hospital for Sick Children Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: The Department of Paediatric Dentistry at The Hospital for Sick Children (SickKids), Faculty of Dentistry, University of Toronto, is inviting applications for the position of Dentist-in-Chief. This is a full-time role for an academic paediatric dentist and leader in paediatric oral health. The effective date of this appointment is June 2027 but can be negotiated. Primary Description Responsibilities include full clinical practice, research, and education, and administrative responsibilities as required by a Department Chief. Evidence of excellence in teaching and research is required. This role reports directly to the Chief of Peri-Operative Services. Additional Description The successful candidate should be a senior academic with an international reputation who can provide effective and collaborative leadership and vision at the hospital, provincial, national and international levels. The candidate must be eligible for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor at the University of Toronto and be eligible for certification with the Royal College of Dental Surgeons of Ontario. Candidates must obtain an appropriate Visa, if applicable. We are seeking an individual whose leadership qualifications include management proficiency, dispute resolution, communications expertise, mentoring capabilities, the ability to engender trust, function collaboratively with all manner of clinical teams and administration. Additionally, the successful candidate will have a long-term vision for the Department which integrates with the mission of Peri-Operative Services, the Hospital and the University of Toronto. Salary will be in the range of $200,000 to $400,000 for 40 hours per week, commensurate with qualifications and experience, and which will be supplemented by clinical billing activity. Benefits include Medical, Dental, Life, Group Insurance, Pension, Vision. A per annum stipend, in recognition of the administrative leadership responsibilities associated with the Department Chief role, will be provided. Support for relocation expenses will be offered. The position has a potential duration of two terms of five years each. Department Description The Department of Paediatric Dentistry at The Hospital for Sick Children consist of 18 dental surgeons, orthodontists and paediatric dentists, provides dental care for children and adolescents under 18 years whose treatment is best managed in a multidisciplinary hospital environment . It is integral to the Ontario Cleft Lip and Palate Craniofacial Dental program and is a major clinical training and research centre for the University of Toronto Graduate Paediatric Dentistry program. The Department also provides training to hospital staff, community dentists, physicians and families through affiliation with the University of Toronto. Hospital Description SickKids, located at 555 University Avenue in downtown Toronto, is among the largest children\u2019s hospitals in North America and has a long history of excellence in clinical care, research and education. It was named the number one paediatric hospital in the world in 2025, by Newsweek magazine. SickKids is fully affiliated with the University of Toronto and is located adjacent to the University campus and to numerous specialty hospitals and biomedical research institutes. Toronto is a vibrant and multicultural city, widely recognized as one of the most diverse cities in the world, with approximately half of its residents born outside Canada and representation from more than 250 ethnicities. This diversity is reflected in the city\u2019s neighbourhoods, food scene, Michelin-starred restaurants across Toronto and the surrounding region, and strengths in key sectors such as life sciences, finance, information, and communications technology. As one of the largest cities in North America, Toronto offers a dynamic downtown, diverse neighbourhoods, strong schools, and access to leading universities. It is regularly recognized in global city, travel, and liveability rankings. World-famous lake and wilderness country and year-round outdoor recreation are easily accessible, as are two hub airports for regional and international travel. Application Process Applications will be accepted until October 30, 2026. Should you be interested in this opportunity, please forward a one-page statement of interest [which includes the names and contact information for 3 referees] and curriculum vitae, in PDF format, via email to: Dr. Abhaya V. Kulkarni Chief, Peri-Operative Services and Chair of the Search Committee EMAIL: dentistrydepartmentchief.recruitment@sickkids.ca All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. For more information about the University of Toronto, Graduate Specialty Program of Paediatric Dentistry, please visit our home page at https://www.dentistry.utoronto.ca Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Clinic, Dental, Physician, Research, Healthcare Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: All Opportunities, Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Assistant%2C-Associate%2C-or-Full-Professor-Adult-Cardiologist-in-Echocardiography-ON/599782317/": {"title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor", "description": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor  - Adult Cardiologist in Echocardiography Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor  - Adult Cardiologist in Echocardiography Date Posted: 02/11/2026 Closing Date: 12/10/2026 , 11:59PM ET Req ID: 46900 Job Category: Faculty - Clinical Faculty/Division: Temerty Faculty of Medicine Department: Dept of Medicine Hospital Affiliation: University Health Network (UHN) & Sinai Health System (SHS) Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: The posting deadline has been extended. The Department of Medicine, Temerty Faculty of Medicine at the University of Toronto, and the Division of Cardiology of the University Health Network (UHN) and Sinai Health System (SHS) are seeking to recruit an academic cardiologist in the area of Echocardiography. The successful candidate must be eligible for a full-time clinical academic appointment at the University of Toronto as a Clinician Investigator at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. The anticipated start date is January 1, 2027, or as mutually agreed upon. The echocardiography laboratory runs a busy program with >35,000 echocardiography studies (inpatients and outpatients) per year that includes a breath of pathologies ranging from valvular heart disease, genetic and infiltrative cardiomyopathies, patients receiving cancer therapy, advanced cardiac support devices (e.g., LVADs, ECMO), congenital heart disease, etc. The laboratory supports the University Health Network and the community. The laboratory consists of 17 faculty readers and over 50 sonographers. It is a multivendor laboratory. The program has an active research portfolio. It supports 3-5 advanced imaging fellows per year and has a strong educational footprint. The primary goal of this recruitment is to participate and enrich the educational and scholarly achievements of the Echocardiography Program. The successful candidate will have demonstrated academic productivity or potential in the field of echocardiography \u2013 through research and/or educational initiatives. They will have completed a level 3 fellowship in echocardiography \u2013 with skill acquisition in advanced echocardiography including contrast, stress, strain, structural imaging and support, transesophageal and 3-D imaging. The clinical position involves the interpretation of both inpatient and outpatient transthoracic and stress imaging (treadmill, bike and pharmacologic); as well as the performance of transesophageal echocardiography. The laboratory also provides educational rotations for the cardiology residents at the University of Toronto as well as a level 3 echocardiography fellowship. Preference will be given to applicants with an academic and research focus aligned with the Peter Munk Cardiac Centre priority areas such as structural valve disease, heart function, cardio-oncology, adult congenital heart disease and/or artificial intelligence. The successful candidate will help to advance technical developments in echocardiography and/or use echocardiographic data to address knowledge gaps and/or help promote education in echocardiography using novel approaches. The successful candidate will report to the Division Director of Cardiology at UHN/SHS. Candidates must hold an MD degree or equivalent and should have, or be eligible for, certification in Cardiology by the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada and licensure with the College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario (CPSO). The primary language of work will be English. Compensation will depend on the amount of clinical work performed (estimated based on fee for service billing), but will be in the usual range for an experienced academic Cardiologist in the Province of Ontario, commensurate with qualifications and experience. Health benefit options and plans are supplied per institutional policy. In addition, the candidate may be reimbursed partial start-up costs for office expenses. Applications will be accepted until December 10, 2026, or until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and interested candidates are encouraged to apply as early as possible. Interested applicants should send a cover letter, a copy of their curriculum vitae and the name and contact information of three referees to: Dr. Douglas Lee Director, Division of Cardiology, UHN/SHS c/o Tiffany La Email: tiffany.la@uhn.ca CAUTION: This ad is \u201cposted only\u201d to the U of T affiliated hospital job board. Please see the information directly above for the application instructions; applications submitted via the \"Apply Now\" button on this page will NOT be considered for this position. For more information about the Department of Medicine at the University of Toronto, please visit our website at https://www.deptmedicine.utoronto.ca . The Department of Medicine\u2019s academic position descriptions can be viewed on this webpage: https://www.deptmedicine.utoronto.ca/academic-position- descriptions . Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Cardiac, Laboratory, Genetics, Medical Technologist, Oncology, Healthcare, Science Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: All Opportunities, Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/TORONTO-Assistant-Professor-Jazz-ON/606723917/": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Assistant Professor - Jazz Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Assistant Professor - Jazz Date Posted: 10/05/2026 Closing Date: 11/16/2026, 11:59PM ET Req ID: 50080 Job Category: Faculty - Tenure Stream (continuing) Faculty/Division: Faculty of Music Department: Faculty of Music Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes POSITION DESCRIPTION The Faculty of Music at the University of Toronto invites applications for a full-time tenure-stream position in the area of Jazz. The appointment will be at the rank of Assistant Professor with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. REQUIREMENTS AND QUALIFICATIONS Candidates must have earned a doctoral degree in Jazz Performance and/or Jazz Composition/Arranging, or a closely related area by the time of appointment, with a demonstrated record of excellence in research (including creative professional activities/practice) and teaching in jazz. We seek candidates whose research and teaching complement and enhance our existing departmental strengths . Candidates must provide evidence of research excellence which can be demonstrated by a record of achievement that could include: performances and/or compositions/arrangements for major venues, organizations, and festivals; collaborations with established artists and ensembles; public presence through broadcasts, recordings, and media platforms; presentations at significant academic or professional conferences; publications in top-ranked and field-relevant journals; awards and accolades; arts and/or research grants; the submitted research statement; and strong endorsements from referees. Evidence of excellence in teaching will be demonstrated through teaching accomplishments and the teaching dossier (including the required materials outlined below, as well as superior performance in other teaching-related activities), and strong letters of reference. Other teaching-related activities can include performance as a teaching assistant, course instructor or ensemble leader, experience leading successful workshops/clinics or seminars, or student mentorship. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be expected to pursue innovative and independent research (including creative professional activities/practice), and to establish an outstanding, competitive, and externally funded research program. In addition, the successful candidate will be assigned a teaching and service load. Teaching responsibilities will include undergraduate and graduate jazz courses, including one-on-one performance lessons and/or leading jazz ensembles, as appropriate to the candidate\u2019s areas of specialization; and research supervision and mentoring of jazz students at the masters and doctoral levels. HIRING SALARY RANGE The expected salary range is $135,000\u2013$160,000 CAD per annum, depending on experience. Negotiations beyond this salary range will be considered for exceptionally qualified candidates. ABOUT US The Faculty of Music is a division of Canada\u2019s largest university, located in one of the world\u2019s most diverse cities. The Faculty of Music offers undergraduate and graduate (MMus and DMA) degrees in Jazz. For further information see music.utoronto.ca and uoftjazz.ca . APPLICATION PROCESS All application materials, including recent reference letters, must be received by November 16, 2026. All qualified candidates are invited to apply online by clicking the link below. Your CV and cover letter should be uploaded into the dedicated fields. Please combine additional application materials into one or two files in PDF/MS Word format. Links to audiovisual documentation submitted as part of the application must remain valid for the duration of the search process. Applicants must submit the following materials: a cover letter; a current curriculum vitae; a statement outlining plans for current and future research (including creative professional activities/practice) a select portfolio of recent samples of their work including stable active links to audiovisual documentation and including description of the applicant\u2019s role in collaborative outputs a teaching dossier, including: a teaching statement sample course materials teaching evaluations or evidence of superior performance in other teaching-related activities as listed above; and the name and contact information of three references. The University of Toronto\u2019s recruiting tool will automatically solicit and collect letters of reference from each referee within 48 hours of an application being submitted. Applicants remain responsible for ensuring that references submit recent letters (on letterhead, dated and signed) by the closing date. More details on the automatic reference letter collection, including timelines, are available in the candidate FAQ . If you have any questions about this position, please contact Dean Ryan McClelland at dean.music@utoronto.ca . All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Audio Visual, Equity, CAD, Drafting, Research, Finance, Creative, Engineering Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: Faculty Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Communications-Coordinator-ON/606723717/": {"title": "Communications Coordinator", "description": "Communications Coordinator Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Communications Coordinator Date Posted: 10/05/2026 Req ID: 50436 Faculty/Division: Faculty of Nursing Department: Lawrence Bloomberg Faculty of Nursing Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Position Number: 00058883 Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About us: The Lawrence Bloomberg Faculty of Nursing at the University of Toronto is a globally recognized leader in nursing education, research, and practice. Our mission is to advance health through a commitment to excellence in education and research. We are dedicated to creating a supportive environment that fosters innovation and collaboration among our faculty, staff, and students. Your opportunity: This role focuses on executing strategic communication initiatives that highlight the Faculty of Nursing\u2019s academic excellence, research impact, and community engagement. It aims to strengthen the faculty\u2019s reputation, attract prospective students, and foster meaningful connections with stakeholders through compelling storytelling and targeted outreach. Under the functional supervision of the Senior Communications and Media Relations Officer, this position plays a key role in implementing the Faculty\u2019s strategic communication plan. The incumbent will be responsible for coordinating project tasks and strategic initiatives with internal and external stakeholders, as well as tracking and monitoring outreach activities. Responsibilities include developing and producing promotional and outreach materials; analyzing and recommending approaches for the visual design and layout of digital assets; creating and maintaining a strong presence across social media platforms; and providing guidance on emerging social media trends to enhance digital communication strategies. The role also ensures that information on all digital platforms remains accurate, current, and aligned with the Faculty\u2019s brand and objectives. Your responsibilities will include: Supporting digital communications activities by coordinating tasks for projects and other strategic initiatives. Responding to requests for visual material, and ensuring consistency and adherence to Faculty and University of Toronto's brand guidelines. Producing content, promotional and outreach materials for a variety of communications and marketing initiatives through print and/or online communications channels. Writing and editing digital communications for print and online channels. Tracking and monitoring social media and outreach activities, including generating reports. Providing website support including liaising with website users regarding usability feedback, creating and editing web pages, and verifying content is up-to-date. Supports social media strategies by creating and maintaining presence on social media platforms and advising on social media trends and platforms to enhance digital communication strategies. Conducting photography and/or videography shoots for a variety of projects. Essential Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree in Communications or Marketing or acceptable combination of equivalent experience. Minimum four years of experience in digital communications, content creation, or marketing. Experience producing visual, promotional, and outreach materials for print and digital platforms. Knowledge and experience managing social media channels, including building active engagement, maintaining a consistent voice and expanding reach. Proficiency with social media platforms such as LinkedIn and Meta platforms, scheduling software (e.g. Buffer, Sprout) and web content management tools (Wordpress). Proficiency with Microsoft 365 applications, Adobe Photoshop, and Canva. Strong planning, project management skills with excellent prioritization and time management skills to meet multiple and conflicting deadlines. Demonstrated ability to work independently, showing initiative and good judgment, and be an effective team member. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills (both verbal and written) with the ability to concisely summarize information. Client-services oriented with demonstrated listening and critical thinking skills. Creative and solutions-oriented with exceptional attention to detail and willingness to learn new skills. Assets(Nonessential): Experience using Adobe Premiere. To be successful in this role you will be: Accountable Adaptable Articulate Communicator Cooperative Organized Problem solver Closing Date: 10/19/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Budget - Continuing Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 09 -- $70,633 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $90,329. Pay scale and job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay Equity Maintenance Protocol. Job Category: Communication/Media/Public Relations Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Marketing Communications, Photography, Communications, PR, Project Manager, Marketing, Creative, Technology Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Assistant-Professor-Climate-and-Community-Health-Equity-ON/606727817/": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Assistant Professor - Climate and Community Health Equity Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Assistant Professor - Climate and Community Health Equity Date Posted: 10/05/2026 Closing Date: 12/03/2026, 11:59PM ET Req ID: 49399 Job Category: Faculty - Tenure Stream (continuing) Faculty/Division: University of Toronto Scarborough Department: University of Toronto Scarborough: Department of Health & Society Campus: University of Toronto Scarborough (UTSC) Existing Vacancy: Yes POSITION DESCRIPTION: The Department of Health and Society (DHS) at the University of Toronto Scarborough (UTSC) invite applications for a full-time tenure stream position in Climate and Community Health Equity. The appointment will be at the rank of Assistant Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. This position part of the Canada Excellence Research Chair\u2019s Network for Equity in Sustainability Transitions (CERC NEST). The CERC NEST uses a transdisciplinary, comparative and action-centred approach with communities and city actors in Toronto and internationally to examine how to enhance participation in decision-making, address inequities in regulations and investments, and ensure fair distribution of benefits, livability and a good life for all. REQUIREMENTS AND QUALIFICATIONS Candidates must have earned a PhD degree in a related discipline (e.g., Sociology, Engineering, Public Health, Environmental Sciences, etc.) by the time of appointment or shortly thereafter with a demonstrated record of excellence in research and teaching. We seek candidates whose research and teaching interests complement and broaden our existing departmental strengths , dovetail with CERC NEST research vision and methodologies, and\u00a0enhance the campus strengths and priorities. The successful candidate will have mixed or multi-methods research experience relevant to sustainability transitions by focusing on the human health and community equity implications of climate-related issues, such as extreme heat, air pollution (indoor and ambient), and energy use in cities. Grounded in the CERC-NEST goal of co-producing knowledge to foster equity in sustainability transitions, this hire will take an integrated approach to community health equity. Teaching experience is essential, and experience in curriculum or course development will be considered an asset. Strong professional, research, and academic networks are also advantageous. Candidates must provide evidence of research excellence which can be demonstrated through a record of publications in top-ranked and field-relevant journals or forthcoming publications that meet high international standards; the submitted research statement; as well as presentations at significant conferences; awards and accolades; and strong endorsements from referees of high standing. Demonstrated teaching ability across a range of courses is required, including areas such as climate change mitigation and adaptation in cities, community health equity, and health across diverse communities throughout the lifespan. Evidence of excellence in teaching will be demonstrated through teaching accomplishments, the teaching dossier (including the required materials outlined below, as well as evidence of superior performance in other teaching-related activities), and strong letters of reference. Other teaching-related activities can include performance as a teaching assistant or course instructor, experience leading successful workshops or seminars, student mentorship, or excellent conference presentations or posters. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. We seek candidates who value diversity and whose research, teaching, and service commit to equity, diversity, inclusion and to the promotion of a respectful and collegial learning and working environment, as demonstrated through the application materials. Candidates are asked to submit a 1\u20132-page statement of contributions to equity and diversity, which might cover topics such as (but not limited to): research or teaching that incorporates a focus on underrepresented communities, the development of inclusive pedagogies, or the mentoring of students from underrepresented groups. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be expected to pursue innovative and independent research, and to establish an outstanding, competitive, and externally funded research program. The successful candidate is also expected to establish strong connections particularly within Scarborough and between DHS, the CERC NEST, and the new Scarborough Academy of Medicine and Integrated Health ( SAMIH ). In addition, the successful candidate will be assigned a teaching and service load. HIRING SALARY RANGE The salary range is $145,000 - $165,000 CAD per annum, depending on experience. Negotiations beyond this salary range will be considered for exceptionally qualified candidates. ABOUT US The University of Toronto offers the opportunity to conduct research and creative practice, teach, and live in one of the most diverse cities in the world. The appointment is at the University of Toronto Scarborough, which is a research-intensive campus with an interdisciplinary commitment, a multicultural student body, and a modern campus. Within the University of Toronto Scarborough, the Department of Health and Society is home to a diverse community of students, teachers, and researchers. Members of our growing department ask cutting-edge questions about health, disability, illness, and disease, locally and globally and from a range of disciplinary perspectives. For more information about the Department of Health and Society at the University of Toronto Scarborough, please visit our website . APPLICATION PROCESS All application materials, including recent reference letters, must be received by December 3 , 2026. All qualified candidates are invited to apply online by clicking the link below. Your CV and cover letter should be uploaded into the dedicated fields. Please combine additional application materials into one or two files in PDF/MS Word format. Applicants must submit the following materials: a cover letter; a current curriculum vitae; a 2- to 3-page research statement outlining current and future research interests; 1 to 3 recent article-length publications; a teaching dossier that includes a strong teaching statement, sample course materials, and teaching evaluations or evidence of superior performance in other teaching-related activities as listed above; an EDI statement as described above; and the name and contact information of three references. The University of Toronto\u2019s recruiting tool will automatically solicit and collect letters of reference from each referee within 48 hours of an application being submitted. Applicants remain responsible for ensuring that referees submit recent letters (on letterhead, dated, and signed) by the closing date. Submission guidelines and details on the automatic reference letter collection, including timelines, are available in the candidate FAQ . If you have any questions about this position, please contact Professor Michelle Silver, Department Chair, at dhs-chair@utsc.utoronto.ca . All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Medical, Sociology, Public Health, Sustainability, Social Sciences, Healthcare, Energy, Research Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: Faculty Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Finance-and-Operations-Assistant-ON/606728917/": {"title": "Finance and Operations Assistant", "description": "Finance and Operations Assistant Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Finance and Operations Assistant Date Posted: 10/05/2026 Req ID: 50432 Faculty/Division: Temerty Faculty of Medicine Department: Department of Psychiatry Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Position Number: 00059739 Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About us: Home to over 40 departments and institutes, the University of Toronto's Temerty Faculty of Medicine lies at the heart of the Toronto Academic Health Science Network and is a global leader in ground-breaking research and education, spanning clinical medicine, basic science and the rehabilitation sciences sectors. Your opportunity: As one of the largest Departments of Psychiatry in the world, we serve as a catalyst for progress, mobilizing partners and resources to improve the outcome of those living with mental illness. We strive to provide the best care to today\u2019s patients and to transform the understanding and treatment of mental illness and addiction for the patients of tomorrow. As Finance and Operations Assistant, you will work closely with the Business Manager to support our department's financial administration, post-award activities, and day-to-day operations. Relying on your relentless attention to detail and strong organizational skills, you will process financial and payroll transactions, monitor and reconcile financial accounts, support post-award administration, and coordinate a range of operational activities that contribute to the effective functioning of the Department. Your responsibilities will include: Act as a primary point of contact for financial inquiries, triage information and maintain an organized physical/digital filing system Pepare, verify, and process accounts payable/receivable, expense reimbursements, invoices, payroll and deposits in accordance with the University Guide to Financial Management Provide operational support for the department, including writing routine documents and correspondence, coordinating supplies and equipment, and supporting other day-to-day operational needs Monitor post-award financial administration activities by maintaining award tracking records, monitoring payment schedules and reporting requirements Generate standard financial reports and gather information to support departmental planning and reporting Essential Qualifications: Advanced College Diploma (3 years) oracceptable combination of equivalent experience Minimum two (2) years relevant financial support experience Demonstrated experience processing financial transactions and preparing financial reconciliations Experience interpreting and applying financial guidelines and procedures Experience maintaining databases, including compiling, sorting and organizing financial data, and generating reports Experience providing operational and administrative support, including maintaining office equipment and supply inventory and coordinating space, facilities, and other day-to-day operational needs Experience supporting post award administration Advanced proficiency with M365, particularly MS Excel, and experience with SAP systems, including FIS and HRIS Excellent communication (written and verbal) and interpersonal skills Strong numerical, accounting, financial and analytical skills, with the ability to analyze information and resolve discrepancies Strong organizational skills and an ability to multi-task on varied duties Ability to accurately interpret and apply guidelines, procedures and regulations in a variety of situations, and the ability to professionally communicate intricate financial procedures Excellent attention to detail and a high degree of accuracy Ability to exercise good judgment and tact in dealing with faculty members, students and staff To be successful in this role youwill be: Communicator Insightful Meticulous Procedural Team player Closing Date: 10/16/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Budget - Continuing Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 07 -- $62,646 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $80,116. Pay scale and job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay Equity Maintenance Protocol. Job Category: Finance/Budget/Planning/Audit Divisional HR Office Email: medhr@utoronto.ca Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Job descriptions are available upon request for internal applicants. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Payroll, Operations Manager, Rehabilitation, Accounts Payable, Accounts Receivable, Finance, Operations, Healthcare Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Graduate-Program-Coordinator-ON/606729317/": {"title": "Graduate Program Coordinator", "description": "Graduate Program Coordinator Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Graduate Program Coordinator Date Posted: 10/05/2026 Req ID: 50423 Faculty/Division: Faculty of Kinesiology & Physical Education Department: Faculty of Kinesiology & Physical Education Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Position Number: 00019187 Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About us: The University of Toronto\u2019s Faculty of Kinesiology and Physical Education is globally recognized for developing and advancing knowledge about the interactions of physical activity and health through outstanding undergraduate and graduate education programs, cutting-edge research and the delivery of sport, recreation and high performance athletic opportunities for students, staff, faculty and community members across the three campuses. In achieving this vision, the Faculty is guided by values of integrity, respect, equity and diversity https://kpe.utoronto.ca/. Your opportunity: The Registrar's Office staff are committed to strong service excellence in supporting the academic and registrarial needs of current undergraduate, graduate and professional master\u2019s students. These services include advising and administration as it relates to registration, financial need, scholarships & awards, and graduation.In addition, the Registrar\u2019s Office is responsible for recruitment and admissions of prospective students, administration of experiential learning opportunities (international, research, in-field learning courses), scheduling of academic courses and exams and providing faculty support. The staff in the Office work collectively toward enhancing the student experience while being committed to operating under principles of equity, diversity and inclusion. The Graduate Program Coordinator is an integral member of a team of administrative and student services professionals who work together to provide exceptional student services in the Faculty of Kinesiology & Physical Education. Reporting to the Assistant Registrar, Graduate Programs, the Graduate Program Coordinator provides day to day program support by answering inquiries from students regarding admission requirements, exam and defence administration, program and tuition information, and funding. The incumbent is responsible for providing support to all KPE graduate students through their programs, ensuring that all Faculty and School of Graduate Studies requirements are met. The Graduate Program Coordinator plays a supporting function for graduate awards processes and competitions, graduate student funding, student registration, petitions & appeals, programs of study, project/thesis requirements, overall degree requirements and eligibility to graduate. Working in collaboration with other members of the Registrar\u2019s Office, the coordinator participates in recruitment initiatives as needed, to attract the best possible candidates for the program. The incumbent will participate in department and Faculty special events which enhance the graduate student experience. Your responsibilities will include: Advising students on academic and/or financial matters by providing options and consequences incorporating personal circumstances impacting academic success and an understanding of an individual's background Assessing and selecting qualified applicants, including exceptional cases Providing detailed information regarding admission requirements to prospective students and applicants Analyzing statistical information to inform admissions projections and planning activities Providing detailed explanations of admissions decisions Promoting programs at recruitment events Essential Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree or acceptable combination of equivalent experience. Minimum of four years of experience in an academic student services environment in a university setting at the graduate studies level working with graduate students and graduate programs, including advising on academic matters, funding opportunities, university regulations, and program requirements Experience dealing with with a diverse multicultural pool of students and students in crisis Strong organizational and time-management skills Ability to multi-task while adhering to strict timelines Strong problem-solving skills; high level accuracy and attention to detail Excellent communication (written and oral); excellent interpersonal and advising skills Excellent database management skills Demonstrated computer skills, including advanced skills in Word, Excel, Access, PowerPoint, and Content Management. Assets (Nonessential): Experience with complex funding Excel spreadsheets an asset To be successful in this role you will be: Accountable Diplomatic Proactive Procedural Resourceful Team player Closing Date: 10/19/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Budget - Continuing Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 12 -- $84,564 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $108,145. Pay scale and job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay Equity Maintenance Protocol. Job Category: Registrarial Services Divisional HR Office Email: Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Job descriptions are available upon request for internal applicants. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Event Planning, Program Manager, Equity, Database, Hospitality, Management, Finance, Research, Technology Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Assistant-Professor%2C-Teaching-Stream-Linguistics-ON/606744617/": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream", "description": "Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream - Linguistics Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream - Linguistics Date Posted: 10/05/2026 Closing Date: 11/30/2026, 11:59PM ET Req ID: 49393 Job Category: Faculty - Teaching Stream (continuing) Faculty/Division: University of Toronto Scarborough Department: University of Toronto Scarborough: Department of Language Studies Campus: University of Toronto Scarborough (UTSC) Existing Vacancy: Yes POSITION DESCRIPTION The Department of Language Studies at the University of Toronto Scarborough (UTSC) invites applications for a full-time teaching stream position in Linguistics. The appointment will be at the rank of Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. This search aligns with the University\u2019s commitment to strategically and proactively promote diversity among our community members ( Statement on Equity, Diversity & Excellence ). Recognizing that Black, Indigenous, and other Racialized communities have experienced inequities that have developed historically and are ongoing, we strongly welcome and encourage candidates from those communities to apply. In addition, the University of Toronto Scarborough is implementing its Strategic Plan: Inspiring Inclusive Excellence . \u00a0Consistent with the values and objectives in that plan, we especially welcome candidates who self-identify as Indigenous or those who have lived experience in Black or other racialized (persons of colour) communities. \u00a0The successful candidate will have the opportunity to be connected with colleagues from under-represented groups, including those hired through targeted efforts in recent years across departments spanning the Sciences, Social Sciences, and Humanities. REQUIREMENTS AND QUALIFICATIONS Applicants must have earned a PhD degree in Linguistics or a related area by the time of appointment, or shortly thereafter, with a demonstrated record of excellence in teaching Linguistics in a degree-granting program. We seek candidates whose teaching interests complement and enhance our existing departmental strengths . Candidates must have teaching experience in a degree-granting program, including lecture preparation and delivery, curriculum development, and development of online material/lectures. Additionally, candidates must possess a demonstrated commitment to excellent pedagogical inquiry and innovative teaching practices such as Experiential Learning, as well as a demonstrated interest in teaching-related scholarly activities in Linguistics.\u00a0 Candidates must also demonstrate the ability to teach a range of class sizes and course levels, and an interest in developing a long-term career in teaching and related scholarly activity in Linguistics. We are looking for a colleague who specializes in sound systems (phonetics/phonology), with preference given to candidates with a secondary specialization in language acquisition, speech-language disorders, or documentation. Evidence of excellence in teaching and a commitment to excellent pedagogical inquiry will be demonstrated through teaching accomplishments, awards and accolades, presentations at significant conferences, the teaching dossier (including the required materials outlined below), and strong letters of reference. Candidates must also show evidence of a commitment to equity, diversity, inclusion, and to the promotion of a respectful and collegial learning and working environment, as demonstrated through the application materials. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be assigned a teaching and service load. HIRING SALARY RANGE The salary range is $115,000 - $135,000 CAD per annum, depending on experience. Negotiations beyond this salary range will be considered for exceptionally qualified candidates. ABOUT US The Department of Language Studies, located at the University of Toronto Scarborough (UTSC), offers a rich selection of undergraduate programs in Linguistics, French, and English & Chinese Translation. Our faculty are internationally recognized scholars whose research encompasses a wide range of fields, including language acquisition, multilingualism, psycholinguistics, translation, discourse analysis, language documentation and revitalization, sociolinguistics, French language and literature, and English and Chinese translation. We are a collaborative and intellectually dynamic academic community where excellence in research and teaching go hand in hand. Our faculty are dedicated to creating an inclusive, engaging, and supportive learning environment that encourages intellectual curiosity, critical inquiry, and experiential learning. Through innovative teaching and active scholarship, we prepare students to become thoughtful, adaptable, and globally engaged graduates. APPLICATION PROCESS All application materials, including recent reference letters, must be received by November 30, 2026. All qualified candidates are invited to apply online by clicking the link below. Your CV and cover letter should be uploaded into the dedicated fields. Please combine additional application materials into one or two files in PDF/MS Word format. Applicants must submit the following materials: a cover letter; a current curriculum vitae; a complete teaching dossier, including a teaching statement, sample syllabi and course materials, and teaching evaluations; and the name and contact information of three references. At least one reference letter must primarily address the candidate\u2019s teaching. The University of Toronto\u2019s recruiting tool will automatically solicit and collect letters of reference from each referee within 48 hours of an application being submitted. Applicants remain responsible for ensuring that references submit recent letters (on letterhead, dated and signed) by the closing date. Submission guidelines and details on the automatic reference letter collection, including timelines, are available in the candidate FAQ . If you have any questions about this position, please contact Beryl Tang at beryl.tang@utoronto.ca or Professor S\u00e9bastien Drouin at dls-chair.utsc@utoronto.ca All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Curriculum Development, Equity, CAD, Drafting, Education, Finance, Research, Engineering Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: Faculty Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Mississauga-Assistant-Professor-Game-Studies-ON/605837017/": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Assistant Professor - Game Studies Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Assistant Professor - Game Studies Date Posted: 09/04/2026 Closing Date: 11/02/2026, 11:59PM ET Req ID: 48803 Job Category: Faculty - Tenure Stream (continuing) Faculty/Division: University of Toronto Mississauga Department: University of Toronto Mississauga: English & Drama Campus: University of Toronto Mississauga (UTM) Existing Vacancy: Yes The Department of English and Drama at the University of Toronto Mississauga (UTM) invites applications for a full-time tenure stream position in the field of Game Studies. The appointment will be at the rank of Assistant Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1st, 2027. This search aligns with the University\u2019s commitment to strategically and proactively promote diversity among our community members ( Statement on Equity, Diversity & Excellence ). Recognizing that Black, Indigenous, and other Racialized communities have experienced inequities that have developed historically and are ongoing, we strongly welcome and encourage candidates from those communities to apply. REQUIREMENTS AND QUALIFICATIONS Candidates must have earned a PhD degree in English, Media Studies, Comparative Literature, Cinema Studies, or a related area by the time of appointment, or shortly thereafter, with a demonstrated record of excellence in research and teaching in Game Studies. We seek candidates whose research and teaching interests complement and enhance our existing departmental strengths . Candidates will have a dynamic humanities-based approach to research and teaching that focuses on storytelling and world-making in digital and analog games and virtual environments. We seek applicants whose work connects the critical analysis of games with the theory and practice of game design. We welcome applicants whose work engages games from critical perspectives including narrative theory, game history, literary studies, media studies, cultural studies, critical race studies, gender and sexuality studies, disability studies, critical technology studies, or related fields. A creative practice in narrative design would also be of value. Candidates must provide evidence of research excellence which can be demonstrated through a record of publications in top-ranked and field-relevant journals or forthcoming publications that meet high international standards; the submitted research statement; presentations at significant conferences; awards and accolades; and strong endorsements from referees. Candidates are also welcome to submit relevant samples of creative work. Evidence of excellence in teaching will be demonstrated through teaching accomplishments, the teaching dossier (including the required materials outlined below), and strong letters of reference. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be expected to pursue innovative and independent research, and to establish an outstanding, competitive, and externally funded research program. In addition, the successful candidate will be assigned a teaching and service load. The successful candidate will hold an undergraduate appointment at UTM and will also become a full member of the graduate faculty of the Graduate Department of English , housed on the St. George campus, which consists of colleagues from the University\u2019s three campuses (UTM, U of T Scarborough, and U of T St. George). Duties will consist of research; teaching undergraduate courses at the Mississauga campus and graduate courses at the St. George campus; the supervision of PhD dissertations; and service contributions at both the undergraduate and graduate level. The successful candidate will also be expected to play a significant role in the development and administration of our Game Studies programs in future years. HIRING SALARY RANGE The expected salary range is $120,000 to $140,000 CAD per annum, depending on experience. Negotiations beyond this salary range will be considered for exceptionally qualified candidates. ABOUT US The successful candidate will join a vibrant intellectual community of world-class scholars at Canada\u2019s leading university. UTM is part of the tri-campus University of Toronto, which actively encourages innovative scholarship, and offers a vibrant research community in Game Studies, a wide range of opportunities for collaborative and interdisciplinary research and teaching, and the excitement of working with a highly diverse student population. UTM is the home of the Syd Bolton Collection, one of the world\u2019s most comprehensive collections of gaming-related research materials. The Greater Toronto Area offers amazing cultural and demographic diversity, one of the highest standards of living in the world, and access to one of the world\u2019s most innovative and dynamic game development industries. For further information on the UTM Department of English and Drama and on the tri-campus Graduate Program in English, see http://www.utm.utoronto.ca/english-drama/ and https://www.english.utoronto.ca/graduate . For more information on working at the University of Toronto, please visit https://www.utoronto.ca/research-innovation/working-at-uoft . At UTM we are committed to fostering an environment of diversity and inclusion. With an enviably\u00a0diverse student body, we especially welcome applications from candidates who identify as Indigenous, Black, or racially visible (persons of colour), and who have experience working with,\u00a0teaching\u00a0or mentoring diverse groups or students. Candidates must\u00a0demonstrate, in their application materials, an ability to foster diversity on campus and within the curriculum or discipline, and must show evidence of a commitment to equity, diversity, inclusion, and the promotion of a respectful and collegial environment. Candidates must submit a statement describing their contributions to equity, diversity, and inclusion, which might cover topics such as (but not limited to): teaching that incorporates a focus on underrepresented communities;\u00a0efforts undertaken to develop inclusive pedagogies, collaboration, and engagement with underrepresented communities; and mentoring of students from underrepresented groups. If you have questions about this statement, please contact Professor Jacob Gallagher-Ross, Chair of the Department of English and Drama, at edassist.utm@utoronto.ca . APPLICATION PROCESS All application materials, including recent reference letters, must be received b y November 2nd, 2026. All qualified candidates are invited to apply online by clicking the link below. Your CV and cover letter should be uploaded into the dedicated fields. Please combine additional application materials into one or two files in PDF/MS Word format. Applicants must submit the following materials: a cover letter; a current curriculum vitae; a research statement outlining current and future research interests; a recent writing sample; statement describing contributions to equity, diversity, and inclusion a teaching dossier, including a teaching statement, sample course materials and teaching evaluations the name and contact information of three references. The University of Toronto\u2019s recruiting tool will automatically solicit and collect letters of reference from each referee within 48 hours of an application being submitted. Applicants remain responsible for ensuring that referees submit recent letters (on letterhead, dated and signed) by the closing date. Submission guidelines and details on the automatic reference letter collection, including timelines, are available in the candidate FAQ . If you have any questions about this position, please contact Professor Jacob Gallagher-Ross, Chair of the Department of English and Drama, at edassist.utm@utoronto.ca . All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Game Designer, CAD, Drafting, Academic, Technology, Engineering, Research, Education Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: Faculty Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Associate-Professor-Professor-Strategic-Management-ON/605843517/": {"title": "Associate Professor / Professor", "description": "Associate Professor / Professor - Strategic Management Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Associate Professor / Professor - Strategic Management Date Posted: 09/04/2026 Closing Date: 10/19/2026, 11:59PM ET Req ID: 50076 Job Category: Faculty - Tenure Stream (continuing) Faculty/Division: Joseph L. Rotman School of Management Department: Joseph L. Rotman School of Management Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes POSITION DESCRIPTION The Rotman School of Management at the University of Toronto invites applications for up to two full-time tenure stream appointments in the Strategic Management Area. The appointment will be at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. REQUIREMENTS AND QUALIFICATIONS Candidates must have earned a PhD degree in management or a cognate discipline with a focus on strategic management (e.g., economics, sociology), with a clearly demonstrated record of excellence in research and teaching. We seek candidates whose research and teaching interests complement and enhance our existing strengths . Candidates must provide evidence of research excellence in strategic management, which can be demonstrated through a record of sustained high-impact contributions and publications in top-ranked and field relevant academic journals in the fields of strategy, sociology or economics, the submitted research statement, an active research pipeline, and strong endorsements from referees. We seek individuals with a sustained record of publication in Strategic Management Journal, Administrative Science Quarterly, Academy of Management Journal/Review, American Economic Review, Quarterly Journal of Economics, Econometrica, Journal of Political Economy, Review of Economic Studies, American Journal of Sociology, Management Science, Organization Science, and journals of similar calibre and standing. Further evidence of research excellence includes presentations at significant conferences, awards and accolades, research presentations at other highly ranked universities, and other noteworthy activities that contribute to the visibility and prominence of the discipline. Applicants must demonstrate excellence in teaching, including a strong demonstrated ability to integrate both theory and practice; evidence of effective communication and presentation skills; a willingness to undertake graduate supervision; and a demonstrated mastery of their subject area. Experience in teaching in a management or management adjacent program is required. Evidence of excellence in teaching will be demonstrated through teaching accomplishments, the teaching dossier and strong letters of reference, and for short-listed candidates, a strong performance during the on-campus seminar presentation. The teaching dossier includes a teaching statement, sample course materials, and teaching evaluations. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate is expected to pursue innovative and independent research at the highest level, sustain an outstanding research program, and publish articles in internationally recognized top-ranked and field relevant academic journals in the field of strategy and/or cognate disciplines. The successful candidate will also be expected to provide research leadership within the Strategic Management Area, including the supervision and mentorship of doctoral students and junior colleagues. The successful candidate will be assigned a teaching and service load. HIRING SALARY RANGE The salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The salary range for this position exceeds $200,000 CAD per annum. ABOUT US U of T is Canada's largest university and has an international research reputation. The Rotman School of Management has a strong research-oriented faculty that is supportive of serious scholarship and is committed to the professional development of its faculty. The Rotman School operates a wide range of degree programs, including an undergraduate Commerce program, a Full-time MBA, a One-year MBA, an Evening MBA, an Executive MBA, a Global Executive MBA, a Global Executive MBA for Healthcare and the Life Sciences, a Master of Management, a Master of Finance, a Master of Financial Risk Management, a Master of Management Analytics, a Master of Management Analytics \u2013 AI for Business Professionals, a Graduate Diploma in Professional Accounting, and a Ph.D. program. The Strategic Management Area provides a stimulating and supportive research environment and a strong commitment to excellence in both research and teaching. Within the University of Toronto, the Strategic Management Area has close ties with the Economics Department and the Sociology Department. All Strategic Management PhD students complete graduate-level courses in at least one of these departments. Rotman is situated in the heart of Canada\u2019s corporate and financial center, and the world\u2019s most diverse city. Rotman\u2019s core values reflect this diversity. For more information about the Rotman School and its core values, please visit: http://www.rotman.utoronto.ca/Connect/AboutRotman/OurValues . APPLICATION PROCESS All application materials, including names and contact information of references, must be received by October 19, 2026. All qualified candidates are invited to apply online by clicking the link below. Your CV and cover letter should be uploaded into the dedicated fields. Please combine additional application materials into one or two files in PDF/MS Word format. Applicants must submit the following materials: a cover letter; a current curriculum vitae; a research statement outlining current and future research interests; up to three representative research papers or publications; a teaching dossier including a teaching statement, sample course materials, and teaching evaluations; and the name and contact information of three references (although these will not be contacted necessarily initially but at later stages of the process). If you have any questions about this position, please contact Professor Joshua Gans, Search Committee Chair at Strategy.Recruiting@Rotman.Utoronto.Ca . The University of Toronto has adopted the AAU Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment in Academia , including the requirement that applicants release personnel information from prior employers regarding sexual misconduct. Full details and requirements can be found here. Artificial intelligence (AI) tools may be used in the initial screening or assessment of applications; however, all final assessments and hiring decisions are made by the search committee members. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Academic, Sociology, Risk Management, Education, Social Sciences, Strategy, Research, Finance Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: Faculty Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Research-Associate-%28Limited-Term%29-ON/604151717/": {"title": "Research Associate (Limited-Term)", "description": "Research Associate (Limited-Term) Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Research Associate (Limited-Term) Date Posted: 07/08/2026 Req ID: 49236 Faculty/Division: UofT Scarborough Department: UTSC:Dept-Physical & Environmental Sci Campus: University of Toronto Scarborough (UTSC) Description: Field and experimental investigation of sulphate-driven changes in carbon and phosphorus cycling in Lake Ontario catchment ecosystems Your Opportunity: Prof. Maria Dittrich, in the Department of Physical and Environmental Sciences at the University of Toronto Scarborough, invites applicants for the position of Research Associate (Limited Term) to conduct research on investigating the effects of sulphate elevation on coupled carbon and phosphorus cycling in aquatic ecosystems of the Lake Ontario catchment. This opportunity for a Research Associate (Limited Term) is a 1-year term appointment at 87.5% FTE. The anticipated start date is August 31, 2026. This search aligns with the University\u2019s commitment to strategically and proactively promote diversity among our community members ( Statement on Equity, Diversity & Excellence ). Recognizing that Black, Indigenous, and other Racialized communities have experienced in equities that have developed historically and are ongoing, we strongly welcome and encourage candidates from those communities to apply. Job Duties: The position focuses on field and laboratory experiments examining the impact of sulphur on phosphorus and carbon dynamics in freshwater sediments. The successful candidate will: Design and execute the field and laboratory experiments on the impact of elevated sulphur content on phosphorus, iron and carbon speciation in lake sediments, geochemical speciation and microbial activities. Develop and refine sequential fractionation techniques for sulphur, iron, manganese, and phosphorus in lake sediment samples and sediment slurries, and link the impacts to microbial activities. Characterize sediment using advanced structural analysis XRD, Rietveld Refinement techniques, TGA/DSC, SEM/EDX, HRTEM, UV/Vis, FTIR and Raman spectroscopy Perform laboratory experiments on sulphur and oxygen isotopes in sulphate and phosphate, testing the hypothesis about the changes in the pathways of changes Collaborate with team members to develop detailed research plans, write proposals, and manage laboratory instruments. Prepare high-quality research reports, publications, and presentations for dissemination in peer-reviewed journals and conferences. Mentor and assist in training graduate and undergraduate students in relevant research techniques. At all times, the individual is expected to support the overall research and teaching missions of M. Dittrich\u2019s research group. Qualifications (Minimum Qualifications): Candidate must have a Ph.D. in Geochemistry, Biogeochemistry, Environmental Sciences, or a related field, with academic research experience in sediment-water interface biogeochemistry of freshwater, coastal, and marine systems, and a minimum of five years of post-PhD research experience in the field of biogeohemistry. Experience in the design of field lake and coastal systems is essential, including planning and executing multi-station sampling campaigns and ship logistics. Experience designing and conducting laboratory experiments, multi-treatment sediment incubation experiments under controlled sulphate gradients to quantify sulphate-phosphorus-iron coupling, benthic flux dynamics, and reactive cycling is required. Demonstrated expertise in stable isotope geochemistry of sulphur and phosphorus in sedimentary systems. This includes multiple sulphur isotope analyses (\u03b4\u00b3\u2074S, \u03b4\u00b3\u00b3S, \u03b4\u00b3\u2076S, \u0394\u00b3\u00b3S) using the distillation line, performing isotopic measurements using GC-IRMS, and analyzing the isotopic data. Demonstrated expertise in phosphorus isotope geochemistry, specifically the oxygen-isotope composition of phosphate (\u03b4\u00b9\u2078O-PO\u2084), with hands-on experience in the extraction and purification of phosphate from sediment phosphorus pools Hands-on expertise in operationally defined sequential extraction techniques for sediment sulphur, phosphorus, iron, and manganese, e.g., acid-volatile sulphide, chromium-reducible sulphur, and total reduced inorganic sulphur speciation; sequential fractionation of phosphorus and iron sediment pools Proven ability in recovery and sub-sampling of intact, undisturbed gravity sediment cores, high-resolution porewater extraction and depth-resolved water-column sampling using Niskin/CTD, YSI, and PAR systems. Practical bench skills relevant to sediment biogeochemistry are also required, including ICP-OES, GC-IRMS, ion chromatography, and spectrophotometric determination of dissolved nutrients. Experience in analysis of microbial community: the ability to process 16S rRNA amplicon sequencing datasets, apply differential abundance and functional prediction approaches, and link microbial community structure and metabolic potential to sediment geochemistry. Proficiency in data analysis is required, including multivariate statistics and the generation of publication-quality figures. Proven strong track record of peer-reviewed research and strong scientific writing supported by a publication record in international peer-reviewed journals. Excellent analytical, problem-solving, and communication skills. Ability to work collaboratively in a team environment and independently on research projects. Travel: Travelling will be required. The Research Associate will have the opportunity to present research at academic conferences. Notes: This is a 87.5% FTE 1-year term appointment, with the possibility of extension, depending on approval of supervisor based on performance and funding. Prorated hours of work: 31.72 hours per week Pro-rated Pay Scale Group and Hiring Rate: R01 - Pro-rated annual salary at 87.5% FTE $46,830 - $87,806.25 All individuals interested in this position must arrange for the following items to be submitted: 1. Cover letter, arguing for one\u2019s suitability for the project and highlighting relevant research experience. 2. Curriculum Vitae. 3. List of publications. 4. Contact details for two (2) references. Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: R01 -- Research Associates (Limited Term): $53,520 - $100,350 \u00a0annually. Any questions regarding this position should be directed to Prof. Maria Dittrich, m.dittrich@utoronto.ca All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply. Closing Date: 08/26/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: Research Associate Appointment Type : Grant - Term Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: R01 -- Research Associates (Limited Term):  $53,520 - $100,350 Job Category: Research Administration & Teaching Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Geology, Equity, Chemistry, Laboratory, SEM, Engineering, Finance, Science, Marketing Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Mississauga-Senior-Development-Officer-ON/606676917/": {"title": "Senior Development Officer Job Details", "description": "Senior Development Officer Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Senior Development Officer Date Posted: 10/05/2026 Req ID: 50402 Faculty/Division: UofT Mississauga Department: UTM: Ofc of Advancement Campus: University of Toronto Mississauga (UTM) Position Number: 00030103 Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About us: U of T Mississauga\u2014the second largest campus of Canada\u2019s top-ranked university and the only research university in Ontario\u2019s booming Peel Region\u2014is one of the world\u2019s great catalysts of human potential. Our employee community hums with the energy of 3,400 faculty, librarians and staff, who power our campus with curiosity, connection and care. We work together to spark life-changing research and innovation, make an indelible mark on the world by building equitable cities and societies, enable healthy lives, create a sustainable future and ignite entrepreneurship. Above all, we prioritize student success, and seek to give our 16,500 students the lift of a lifetime through learning and discovery. We love to open opportunities available nowhere else for our community to achieve their ambitions and make their unique contributions to Canada and the world. This work all comes together on a spectacularly green campus, alongside the Missinihe-ziibi (Trusting Creek or Credit River), where we seek to honour truth, reciprocity and reconciliation on the traditional land of the Huron-Wendat, Seneca and the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation. We\u2019re thrilled to welcome those who want to learn more about us and to be part of what we do. Your opportunity: As Senior Development Officer (SDO) you will be a key member of a team of dynamic advancement professionals working under the general direction of the Director of Development. You will also join a distinguished group of specialists who are devoted to the highest standards of work and motivated to advance the mission of the University of Toronto. As a U of T employee, you will enjoy the many benefits that come from working at an institution consistently ranked as one of Canada\u2019s top 100 employers. As SDO you will be responsible for identifying and cultivating prospective donors at the major gift level. Drawing on your strong relationship building and management skills you will maintain a robust portfolio of donors and prospective donors (individuals and organizations). You will be responsible for solicitations and ensuring proper stewardship takes place following a gift to maintain the donor's interest and support. You will be involved in training and working alongside volunteers in fundraising activities. As the incumbent, you will work closely with other members of the UTM Advancement team, academic leaders, senior volunteers, and Division of University Advancement colleagues to structure gift opportunities and realize the success of the fundraising program. You will be comfortable working in a fast-paced and high-performance environment as the Defy Gravity campaign continues to reach new heights. You will be an individual passionate about working at Canada\u2019s top university, dedicated to inclusive excellence, and driven by a commitment to making a positive impact. Your responsibilities will include: Identifying prospective donors at the major gift level ($50,000 and above); designing and executing the necessary steps to bring a prospective donor to make a major financial commitment to the University Collaborating closely with academic leaders to identify and construct compelling funding opportunities Meeting face-to-face with current and prospective donors to discuss giving interests and funding proposals Working closely with faculty members and volunteers on appropriate prospective donor solicitation strategies Managing donor relations by developing and implementing customized stewardship plans and strategies to sustain donor engagement Writing donor-facing materials such as funding proposals, acknowledgements and recognition communications; drafting briefing notes, solicitation plans and call reports Essential Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree or acceptable combination of equivalent experience. Minimum five years experience in a staff fundraising role with proven track record of success in managing a substantial pool of major gift donors and prospective donors Demonstrated experience in the identification, cultivation, solicitation of donors and prospective donors, preferably at the $50,000+ level Proven ability to manage and motivate high net worth prospective donors, alumni and volunteers Exceptional interpersonal, spoken and written communication skills Evidence of strong initiative, tact, diplomacy, judgement and decision making in complex environments with competing priorities Strong organizational and analytical skills; keen attention to detail Ability to work in a fast-paced and results-oriented environment Effective team player who values collaboration To be successful in this role you will be: Accountable Communicator Goal oriented Resilient Self-directed Tactful Closing Date: 10/26/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Budget - Continuing Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 15 -- $101,242 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $129,471. Pay scale and job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay Equity Maintenance Protocol. Job Category: Administrative / Managerial Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Job descriptions are available upon request for internal applicants. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Sustainability, Equity, Fundraising, Energy, Nonprofit, Research, Finance Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Manufacturing-Technologist-%288-Month-Term%29-ON/606867217/": {"title": "Manufacturing Technologist (8-Month Term) Job Details", "description": "Manufacturing Technologist (8-Month Term) Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Manufacturing Technologist (8-Month Term) Date Posted: 10/08/2026 Req ID: 50489 Faculty/Division: Faculty of Applied Science & Engineering Department: Dept of Mechanical & Industrial Eng Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Position Number: 00060165 Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About us: The Department of Mechanical & Industrial Engineering is home to about 60 professors leading research on a very broad range of topics. On the Industrial Engineering side, research areas include Operations Research, Information Engineering, Human Factors, and Applied Machine Learning, all of which seek to improve the systems we as humans rely on to navigate our world. On the Mechanical Engineering side, research areas include Robotics, Mechanics & Design, Materials, and Thermofluids, topics that are applied to applications including manufacturing, energy production, and bioengineering. We are also home to about 2,000 very talented students: about 1,300 undergraduates are enrolled in the Industrial Engineering and in the Mechanical Engineering BASc programs. We have about 350 Master of Engineering (MEng) students, a professional program for students taking courses with us for a year or two. And we have about 350 MASc and PhD students, who work individually on research projects with a particular professor. The Department of Mechanical & Industrial Engineering prides itself on being a good place to work: respectful, accepting of difference and professional. We value employees who are goal-oriented and self-motivated, and who are constantly looking to improve how we serve our students and faculty. We believe in teamwork, in working together for the greater good, and yet doing so in a way that respects each employee\u2019s work-life balance. Finally, we value each employee as an individual, and look for opportunities to recognize each contribution. Your opportunity: The machine shop staff design, machine, fabricate, install, maintain, and repair unique scientific devices, components and precision instruments for research purposes and undergraduate projects. The incumbent will work with state-of-the-art software and equipment to implement new processes for the efficient use computer numerically controlled and manual machining equipment which are used to create custom projects that support and enable groundbreaking research at the faculty. Learn more at: https://www.mie.utoronto.ca/student-life-services/machine-shop-professional/ Under general direction of the Machine Shop Manager, the Manufacturing Technologist is responsible for coordinating the installation and implementation of new equipment and processes in the machine shop. They will analyze applicable installation methods for apparatus and devices to suggest and implement improvements to processes and workflows. This may involve designing, modifying and fabricating custom fixtures, tooling and databases. Once implemented, the incumbent will create training materials and standard operating procedures for the unit. Your responsibilities will include: Preparing, assembling and setting-up material, equipment and supplies according to the needs of the facility Providing detailed explanations and demonstrating procedures to students and/or employees on experiment set ups and lab techniques Analyzing and recommending suitable materials and methods of approaching fabrication and/or lab work Analyzing applicable installation methods for apparatus and devices Designing, modifying and fabricating custom lab apparatus, tools and jigs Performing complex or scientific diagnostic tests Implementing plans and process improvements for lab/workshop activities Analyzing and recommending suitable materials and methods of approaching fabrication and/or lab work Essential Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree in Engineering, Mechanical Engineering Technology or Manufacturing Engineering Technology, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum five (5) years of relevant experience installing and implementing machining equipment and supporting continuous improvement, process optimization, or operational efficiency initiatives. Formal training in SolidWorks with at least 2 years' experience. SolidWorks Professional Certification in Design. Experience in configuring and setting up of computer numerically controlled machines(CNC) and computer aided design (CAD/CAM) to produce custom machined and fabricated components. Demonstrate a strong understanding of modern machining and work holding methods. Experience with Microsoft Power Automate to implement automated process flows and data collection. Demonstrated ability to build and nurture strong professional relationships with internal and external stakeholders. Excellent oral, written communication and presentation skills, with the proven ability to explain complex processes to a wide range of audiences. Proven ability to organize, prioritize, manage and meet multiple competing deadlines with high flexibility, accuracy and attention to detail. Strong interpersonal and problem-solving skills with the demonstrated capability to effectively work independently, within a team, and under direction. Proven ability to practice strong professionalism, tact, discretion, initiative, confidentiality, and sound judgment. Demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, inclusion and the promotion of a respectful and collegial learning and work environment. To be successful in this role you will be: Adaptable Goal oriented Motivated self-learner Organized Problem solver Responsible Closing Date: 10/19/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Budget - Term Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 13 -- $89,794 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $114,832. Pay scale and job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay Equity Maintenance Protocol. Job Category: Engineering / Technical Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Job descriptions are available upon request for internal applicants. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: CAD, Drafting, Bioengineering, Biomedical Engineering, Biotech, Engineering, Science Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Executive-Assistant-&-Academic-Appointments-Coordinator-ON/606868617/": {"title": "Executive Assistant & Academic Appointments Coordinator", "description": "Executive Assistant & Academic Appointments Coordinator Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Executive Assistant & Academic Appointments Coordinator Date Posted: 10/08/2026 Req ID: 50488 Faculty/Division: Temerty Faculty of Medicine Department: Department of Pharmacology and Toxicology Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Position Number: 00056146 Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About us: Home to over 40 departments and institutes, the University of Toronto's Temerty Faculty of Medicine lies at the heart of the Toronto Academic Health Science Network and is a global leader in ground-breaking research and education, spanning clinical medicine, basic science and the rehabilitation sciences sectors. Your opportunity: The Department of Pharmacology & Toxicology is among the oldest in North America. We are one of the top Pharmacology and Toxicology Departments in the world due to our high quality research and education. We offer training in pharmacology and toxicology to both undergraduate and graduate students who may subsequently go on to do exciting research, regulatory and administrative careers in academic, industrial, and healthcare provision settings. As Executive Assistant & Academic Appointments Coordinator, you will ensure the effective and efficient operation of the Chair's Office while providing high-quality administrative support for academic searches, appointments, tenure, promotion, and review processes. Working closely with faculty members, academic leaders, committees, and stakeholders, you will coordinate a broad range of academic and administrative activities that require exceptional attention to detail, sound judgment, discretion, and the ability to manage competing priorities in a fast-paced environment. Your ability to interpret policies, coordinate complex processes, and maintain confidential information will help support the Department's academic mission and strategic priorities. Your responsibilities will include: Providing executive and administrative support to the Chair, including managing complex calendars, coordinating meetings and correspondence, monitoring deadlines, arranging travel, preparing materials, and acting as a first point of contact for enquiries Coordinating academic search and appointment activities, including supporting faculty recruitment processes, tracking applications, coordinating interviews and academic presentations, managing recruitment documentation, collecting committee feedback, and preparing appointment-related materials Supporting tenure, promotion, and academic review processes, including collecting and managing documentation, maintaining confidential records, coordinating committee review activities, preparing reports and summaries, and ensuring adherence to established policies, procedures, and deadlines Supporting communications and information management activities, including drafting and editing correspondence, newsletters, websites, handbooks, and other communications materials; maintaining records and databases; and preparing reports and statistical summaries Coordinating committee and operational activities, including preparing agendas and minutes, tracking action items, supporting committee processes, coordinating departmental event logistics and travel arrangements, processing expense reimbursements, and providing guidance on administrative procedures and practices Essential Qualifications: Advanced College Diploma (3 years) or an acceptable combination of equivalent experience Minimum three (3) years of related experience in a senior administrative role, preferably in a university, healthcare, or research environment Demonstrated experience providing executive-level administrative support, including managing complex calendars, coordinating meetings, and supporting senior leaders Demonstrated experience supporting academic searches, appointments, promotion, tenure, review, or other faculty affairs processes Experience coordinating committees, including agenda preparation, minute taking, and follow-up on action items Experience drafting, editing, proofreading, and formatting correspondence, reports, presentations, and communications materials Experience coordinating events, interviews, travel arrangements, and academic programming activities Experience maintaining records, databases, and confidential information with a high degree of accuracy and discretion Experience interpreting and applying policies, procedures, and guidelines, and providing guidance on their application Experience maintaining websites, digital platforms, or online information resources Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, Outlook, and PowerPoint) and virtual meeting platforms Strong organizational and time-management skills with the ability to manage multiple competing priorities and deadlines Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to communicate and summarize complex information clearly and concisely Strong problem-solving skills, attention to detail, and ability to exercise tact, discretion, sound judgment, and confidentiality Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment Assets (Nonessential): Familiarity with Temerty Faculty of Medicine and affiliated hospital policies and procedures Experience with document management systems such as Laserfiche and applicant tracking systems such as SuccessFactors To be successful in this role you will be: Communicator Meticulous Organized Responsible Tactful Closing Date: 10/19/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Budget - Continuing Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 10 -- $75,004 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $95,915. Pay scale and job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay Equity Maintenance Protocol. Job Category: Administrative / Managerial Divisional HR Office Email: medhr@utoronto.ca Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Job descriptions are available upon request for internal applicants. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Copy Editor, Proofreading, Rehabilitation, Toxicology, Administrative Assistant, Creative, Healthcare, Science, Administrative Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Technical-Specialist-ON/606886217/": {"title": "Technical Specialist", "description": "Technical Specialist Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Technical Specialist Date Posted: 10/08/2026 Req ID: 50497 Faculty/Division: Faculty of Applied Science & Engineering Department: APSC Admin. Units Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Description: About us: The Faculty of Applied Science & Engineering is a world-renowned community of researchers and students dedicated to solving some of the world's most pressing challenges through collaborative and multidisciplinary research and experiential education. Through rigorous technical training, and unparalleled extracurricular and professional experience opportunities, we prepare the next generation of engineering leaders and change makers to unlock the future's boundless potential. The Faculty's Information Technology team works together to support a dedicated and diverse group of academic and administrative professionals who exemplify excellence in their fields. Your opportunity: Reporting to the Manager, Client Services, the Technical Specialist will provide senior technical expertise responsible for IT service operations stewardship through planning, implementation, and ongoing support. The incumbent will help cultivate and build positive relationships with staff and departments, working closely with peers, technical teams, and clients to lead and drive sustainable modernization paths for the delivery of high caliber IT solutions and secure computing, focusing on lifecycle management, maintenance, data analytics, and IT service maturity. The incumbent will oversee all aspects of service delivery, including resolving complex issues, major incidents, and provide exemplary hands-on technical support. Your responsibilities will include: Responding to end-user service requests Troubleshooting complex computer system problems Providing consultative advice on IT issues Delegating tasks to IT support staff when appropriate Analyzing service delivery and/or internal processes and recommending improvements Probing for information from end-users to identify problems and establish needs Planning and implementing components of IT related projects Conducting needs assessments to determine end-user technical requirements Essential Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, or related discipline, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum four years of diverse IT experience in a multi-client and multifaceted operational environment. Minimum one year of experience coordinating the activities of and providing orientation to others on work procedures and practices, including assisting in solving complex problems, and providing day-to-day work coordination, scheduling and prioritization. Must have diverse application experience including, but not limited to, standard Office 365 applications, e-mail applications, Microsoft operating systems, web browsers and a desire to expand existing skill sets. Demonstrated experience with MacOS, MS Windows, and Linux/Unix operating systems; iOS ; Web/HTML skills. Experience with installing and configuring endpoint devices with applied security measures. Demonstrated extensive experience writing and disseminating technical or procedural specifications and client facing documentation; skilled at developing key messaging and talking points. Demonstrated experience in analyzing complex problems and producing actionable plans and solutions. Demonstrated experience with conveying complex technical ideasand teaching clients howto use IT systems, including presentation skills with the ability to facilitate staff training sessions, client workshops and meetings. Demonstrated experience in creating complex technical documentation. Demonstrated ability to lead project delivery to completion. Ability to work with data including querying (SQL), report formatting and records creation. Expert level knowledge of all aspects that supports the client experience, including end-user devices setup and configuration, software like Microsoft 365 and its associated application suite and enterprise management tools. Top-tier customer service skills. Ability to communicate effectively and accurately, both verbally and in writing, especially in conveying complex technical ideas to clients, management, and other IT staff members. Ability to troubleshot network connectivity and performance issues. Demonstrated ability to learn on the job, and on own initiative, to keep abreast of rapid changes in a dynamic environment. Strong time management and organizational skills. Assets (Nonessential): Technical certifications related to project management, processimprovement, and business analysis (e.g., PMP, ITIL). Experience with ServiceNow. Experience with Microsoft Intune or KACE Endpoint Systems Management Appliances. Familiarity with the University environment, governance, and policies. To be successful in this role you will be: Accountable Achievement oriented Communicator Multi-tasker Possess a positive attitude Proactive Self-directed Team player Closing Date: 10/20/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Budget - Continuing Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 12 -- $84,564 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $108,145. Pay scale and job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay Equity Maintenance Protocol. Job Category: Information Technology (IT) Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Job descriptions are available upon request for internal applicants. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Fundraising, Business Analyst, Computer Science, Project Manager, Research, Nonprofit, Technology Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Assistant-Manager%2C-Open-Recreation-ON/606886017/": {"title": "Assistant Manager, Open Recreation", "description": "Assistant Manager, Open Recreation Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Assistant Manager, Open Recreation Date Posted: October 8, 2026 Req ID: 50467 Faculty/Division: Faculty of Kinesiology & Physl Ed. Department: Faculty of Kinesiology & Physl Ed. Campus: St. George (downtown) Campus Position Number: 30432 Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: About us: The University of Toronto\u2019s Faculty of Kinesiology and Physical Education is globally recognized for developing and advancing knowledge about the interactions of physical activity and health through outstanding undergraduate and graduate education programs, cutting-edge research and the delivery of sport, recreation and high performance athletic opportunities for students, staff, faculty and community members across the three campuses. In achieving this vision, the Faculty is guided by values of integrity, respect, equity and diversity. kpe.utoronto.ca Your opportunity: Under the general direction of the Director, Active Healthy Living, you will provide professional and dynamic leadership to the Faculty\u2019s Physical Activity and Open Recreation programs across all KPE facilities. You will assist in the development, direction and implementation of comprehensive broad-based programs and events for physical activity, sport and recreation. You will also work to support partners and student leaders in relation to events and activities associated with the University\u2019s MoveU physical activity engagement strategy particularly the programs on the St. George campus. You will support the delivery of drop-in and registered physical activity programs. Your responsibilities will include: Planning and implementing components of curricular and/or co-curricular programs and activities, such as Student Group Access program and the Move U program. Working across the Tri-Campuses, supporting programs facilitated at St George campus (downtown), as well as UTM and UTSC Planning and overseeing the implementation of an evaluation framework for programs and initiatives Analyzing and making recommendations for student program enhancements Ensuring others adhere to established risk management protocols during University events Coordinating the recruitment process and training of casual staff. Directing the activities of casual staff. Assigning and checking the work of work-study students. Fostering cooperative working relationships with a wide variety of internal and external contacts Assist with scheduling and planning for recreational activities, including aquatic activities e.g., drop-in swim Collecting supporting documentation required for payroll and/or HRIS processing Essential Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree in sport management, physical education or kinesiology or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum four years\u2019 experience in physical activity programming, leadership or sport management preferably in a university athletic/recreation setting. Experience supervising staff in a unionized environment. Experience with physical activity, sport and recreations programs for a wide range of clients. Standard First Aid Basic CPR Excellent written and communication skills Strong time management and interpersonal skills Strong computer competencies with Microsoft Office Suite Ability to interact professionally and effectively with people at all levels Understand current physical activity programs and trends that influence UofT programs Ability to balance competing demands of routine work with the special requirements of projects Ability to exercise sound judgment and tact Assets (Nonessential): CanFit Pro Certification, ACSM, CPTN, NCCP Level 2, Fitness Certification, Certified Fitness Consultant through CSEP-CPT Fusion recreation management software experience/expertise Scheduling/facility booking experience An understanding and commitment to diversity and equity issues To be successful in this role you will be: Approachable Communicator Organized Problem solver Team player Closing Date: October 19, 2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: USW Appointment Type : Budget- continuing Schedule: Full-time. Some evening and weekend work. Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: USW Pay Band 12 -- $84,564 with an annual step progression to a maximum of $108,145. Pay scaleand job class assignment is subject to determination pursuant to the Job Evaluation/Pay\u00a0EquityMaintenance Protocol. Job Category: Athletics Lived Experience Statement Candidates who are members of Indigenous, Black, racialized and 2SLGBTQ+ communities, persons with disabilities, and other equity deserving groups are encouraged to apply, and their lived experience shall be taken into consideration as applicable to the posted position. Job descriptions are available upon request for internal applicants. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a\u00a0culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively\u00a0address and serve the interests of our global community. We\u00a0strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples,\u00a0Black and racialized persons, women, persons with\u00a0disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities.\u00a0We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to\u00a0equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse\u00a0perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to\u00a0strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Payroll, Risk Management, Assistant Manager, Equity, HRIS, Finance, Management, Human Resources Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: USW Staff Appointed Opportunities, Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Associate-Professor-Professor-Global-Economics-ON/606817817/": {"title": "Associate Professor / Professor", "description": "Associate Professor / Professor - Global Economics Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Associate Professor / Professor - Global Economics Date Posted: 10/07/2026 Closing Date: 11/30/2026, 11:59PM ET Req ID: 49697 Job Category: Faculty - Tenure Stream (continuing) Faculty/Division: Faculty of Arts & Science Department: Munk School of Global Affairs and Public Policy Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes POSITION DESCRIPTION The Munk School of Global Affairs & Public Policy in the Faculty of Arts & Science at the University of Toronto invites applications for a full-time tenure stream position in the area of Global Economics. The appointment will be at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. REQUIREMENTS AND QUALIFICATIONS Applicants must have earned a PhD in Economics, with a clearly demonstrated record of excellence in research and teaching . The Committee encourages applications from across the discipline but\u00a0is particularly interested in scholars whose work engages with economic policy and/or reflects a deep understanding of global economics. We seek candidates whose research and teaching interests complement and enhance our existing strengths. Candidates must provide evidence of research excellence which can include a record of sustained high-impact publications in top-ranked and/or field-relevant academic outlets, the submitted research statement, presentations at significant conferences, awards or accolades, other noteworthy activities that contribute to the visibility and prominence of the discipline, and strong endorsements from referees of high standing. Evidence of excellence in teaching will be demonstrated through teaching accomplishments, the teaching dossier (including the required materials outlined below) and strong letters of reference. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be expected to sustain, lead, and publish innovative and independent scholarly research, and to maintain an outstanding, competitive, and externally funded research program. In addition, the successful candidate will be assigned a service and teaching load that includes teaching economics to students from a wide range of academic backgrounds in the Munk School\u2019s undergraduate and professional graduate programs (the Master of Global Affairs and the Master of Public Policy ). HIRING SALARY RANGE The rank and salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The salary range for this position exceeds $200,000 CAD per annum. ABOUT US The Munk School of Global Affairs & Public Policy at the University of Toronto is a leader in interdisciplinary research, teaching and global engagement. Established in 2010 through a landmark gift by Peter and Melanie Munk , the Munk School's mission is to be a leader in contributing innovative ideas that help to solve major issues facing the global community. APPLICATION PROCESS All application materials, including names and contact information of references, must be received by November 30th, 2026 . All qualified candidates are invited to apply online by clicking the link below. Your CV and cover letter should be uploaded into the dedicated fields. Please combine additional application materials into one or two files in PDF/MS Word format. Applicants must submit a cover letter; a current curriculum vitae; a research statement outlining current and future research interests; a recent writing sample; a teaching dossier with a teaching statement, sample course materials, and teaching evaluations; and the name and contact information of three references . The Department will request letters of recommendation from references at a later stage of the search process. Equity, diversity and inclusion are essential to academic excellence as articulated in University of Toronto\u2019s Statement on Equity, Diversity and Excellence . We seek candidates who share these values and who demonstrate throughout the application materials their commitment and efforts to advance equity, diversity, inclusion, and the promotion of a respectful and collegial learning and working environment. If you have any questions about this position, please contact Amanda Martin, Academic Administrative Coordinator, at amandas.martin@utoronto.ca . The University of Toronto has adopted the AAU Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment in Academia , including the requirement that applicants release personnel information from prior employers regarding sexual misconduct. Full details and requirements can be found here . All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces diversity and is building a culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively address and serve the interests of our global community. We strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples, Black and racialized persons, women, persons with disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities. We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Public Policy, Equity, Academic, Government, Legal, Research, Finance, Education Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: Faculty Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Assistant-Professor-Global-Economics-ON/606817417/": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Assistant Professor - Global Economics Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert \u00d7 Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now \u00bb Assistant Professor - Global Economics Date Posted: 10/07/2026 Closing Date: 11/30/2026, 11:59PM ET Req ID: 49696 Job Category: Faculty - Tenure Stream (continuing) Faculty/Division: Faculty of Arts & Science Department: Munk School of Global Affairs and Public Policy Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes POSITION DESCRIPTION The Munk School of Global Affairs & Public Policy in the Faculty of Arts & Science at the University of Toronto invites applications for a full-time tenure stream position in the area of Global Economics. The appointment will be at the rank of Assistant Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. REQUIREMENTS AND QUALIFICATIONS Applicants must have earned a PhD in Economics by the time of the appointment, with a demonstrated record of excellence in research and teaching . The Committee encourages applications from across the discipline but\u00a0is particularly interested in scholars whose work engages with economic policy and/or reflects a deep understanding of global economics. We seek candidates whose research and teaching interests complement and enhance our existing strengths. Candidates must provide evidence of research excellence which can include a record of sustained high-impact publications in top-ranked and/or field-relevant academic outlets, the submitted research statement, awards or accolades, and strong endorsements from referees of high standing. Evidence of excellence in teaching will be demonstrated through teaching accomplishments, the teaching dossier (including the required materials outlined below) and strong letters of reference. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be expected to pursue, produce and publish innovative and independent scholarly research, and to establish an outstanding, competitive, and externally funded research program. In addition, the successful candidate will be assigned a service and teaching load that includes teaching economics to students from a wide range of academic backgrounds in the Munk School\u2019s undergraduate and professional graduate programs (the Master of Global Affairs and the Master of Public Policy ). HIRING SALARY RANGE The expected salary range is $130,000 to $170,000 CAD per annum, depending on experience. Negotiations beyond this salary range will be considered for exceptionally qualified candidates. ABOUT US The Munk School of Global Affairs & Public Policy at the University of Toronto is a leader in interdisciplinary research, teaching and global engagement. Established in 2010 through a landmark gift by Peter and Melanie Munk , the Munk School's mission is to be a leader in contributing innovative ideas that help to solve major issues facing the global community. APPLICATION PROCESS All application materials, including recent reference letters, must be received by November 30th, 2026. All qualified candidates are invited to apply online by clicking the link below. Your CV and cover letter should be uploaded into the dedicated fields. Please combine additional application materials into one or two files in PDF/MS Word format. Applicants must submit a cover letter; a current curriculum vitae; a research statement outlining current and future research interests; a recent writing sample; a teaching dossier with a teaching statement, sample course materials, and teaching evaluations; and the name and contact information of three references . The University of Toronto\u2019s recruiting tool will automatically solicit and collect letters of reference within 48 hours of an application being submitted. Applicants remain responsible for ensuring that referees submit recent letters (on letterhead, dated and signed) by the closing date. Submission guidelines and details on the automatic reference letter collection, including timelines, are available in the candidate FAQ . Equity, diversity and inclusion are essential to academic excellence as articulated in University of Toronto\u2019s Statement on Equity, Diversity and Excellence . We seek candidates who share these values and who demonstrate throughout the application materials their commitment and efforts to advance equity, diversity, inclusion, and the promotion of a respectful and collegial learning and working environment. If you have any questions about this position, please contact Amanda Martin, Academic Administrative Coordinator, at amandas.martin@utoronto.ca . All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces diversity and is building a culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively address and serve the interests of our global community. We strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples, Black and racialized persons, women, persons with disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities. We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Public Policy, Equity, Drafting, Government, Legal, Research, Finance, Engineering Apply now \u00bb Find similar jobs: Faculty Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"}, "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Associate-Professor-Professor-Applied-Economics-and-Munk-Chair-in-Economics-ON/606818717/": {"title": "Associate Professor / Professor", "description": "Associate Professor / Professor - Applied Economics and Munk Chair in Economics Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian 